vm-8 — Bytecode fasl: serialize compiled functions (plan-2-bytecode-vm).

Turn plan-1's compact fasl into a BYTECODE fasl — the load-speed payoff.

WHAT
- Extend the cfasl object serializer (cfasl-2) with a COMPILED-FUNCTION tag encoding
  { name, lambda-list, code octet-vector, constant pool (recursively serialized;
  may contain nested compiled functions for FLET/LABELS/closures templates),
  arity/&opt/&rest/&key descriptor, max-stack, n-locals }.  Closures' captured cells
  are NOT serialized (they are created at load when the top-level form runs); only
  function TEMPLATES are.
- COMPILE-FILE: after %COMPILE-FILE-TOPLEVEL (compile-time effects) and
  MACROEXPAND-ALL, COMPILE each top-level form (or wrap it so its lambdas compile)
  to bytecode, and serialise the bytecode form via cfasl.  DEFUN/DEFMETHOD bodies
  become compiled functions.  Top-level effectful forms compile to a thunk that is
  called at load.
- LOAD (cfasl-3): deserialize compiled-function objects and install them (no
  re-compilation, no macroexpansion) — this is the big win: ASDF load stops being
  eval-of-trees and becomes install-of-bytecode.
- COMPILE (in-memory): produce a compiled function directly (already the plan for
  the interactive path).
- LOAD-TIME-VALUE: evaluate at load, patch into the constant pool.

ACCEPTANCE: compile a multi-file system to bytecode fasls, load them, and run —
faster load AND faster run than the tree-walker; round-trip a corpus; suite green.
Benchmark ASDF (require :asdf) with bytecode fasls vs the numbers from cfasl-5.

DEPENDS ON: cfasl-1..4 (plan-1), vm-1..6 (compiler emits functions).

====================================================================
PROGRESS (v1.8.10, branch vm) — PART 1: byte-code fasl SERIALIZATION done.

The cfasl object serializer now round-trips a byte-code CompiledFunction TEMPLATE.

- New tag CFASL_TAG_COMPILED_FUNCTION (sources/cfasl.{h,c}; CFASL_VERSION -> 2).  The
  writer records the template's id BEFORE its fields (sharing-safe), writes the
  BYTECODE_VERSION, then the 8 slots: name, lambda-list, code (the octet STRING),
  constants (the pool — recursively serialized, so NESTED templates for FLET / closures
  round-trip), arity, max-stack, n-locals, source.  The reader reserves the id, checks
  BYTECODE_VERSION (refuses a mismatch), reads the fields, and rebuilds via
  compiled_function_new (reserve-id + patch, like SYMBOL/KEYWORD).  Closures' captured
  cells are NOT serialized (only templates), per the design.
- Verified: tests/test_misc.c::test_vm_fasl — a plain fn, a closure-making fn (nested
  template in the pool), and an extended-lambda-list fn (&key) each round-trip through
  cfasl and run identically.  Full suite green.

REMAINING for this issue (PART 2): wire the pipeline —
- COMPILE (in-memory): make (COMPILE nil '(lambda ...)) / (COMPILE 'name) produce a
  byte-code function via %VM-COMPILE, with a SAFE fallback to the current minimal
  compilation for forms the VM can't yet handle.
- COMPILE-FILE: compile top-level DEFUN/DEFMETHOD bodies (and effectful forms as load
  thunks) to byte-code and serialise via cfasl; LOAD then installs templates with no
  re-compilation (the ASDF load-speed payoff).
- LOAD-TIME-VALUE patched into the pool at load.
BLOCKER for the global COMPILE wiring: the CONDITION SYSTEM.  HANDLER-BIND is a kernel
special operator (eval.c) but is NOT in SPECIAL-OPERATOR-P's list, so the analyzer
treats (handler-bind ...) / (handler-case ...) / (ignore-errors ...) as ordinary calls
and the handler is never established — a function using them would COMPILE but misbehave
at runtime.  Wiring COMPILE globally must wait until either the VM implements the
condition-system special operators (analyzer + handler-frame opcodes, building on
vm-5's NLX) OR COMPILE detects those forms and falls back.  (See the vm-5 closing note.)
